## a b s t r a c t
Metal-organic frameworks are a new type of catalyst precursor with high specific surface area and controllable composition, which can be modified by post-treatment and are suitable for use as cathode catalysts for Zn-air batteries (ZABs). Here, a self-doped nitrogen nanocatalyst $(N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}})$ with a double-layered porous structure is rationally designed for flexible solid-state ZABs. The outer porous carbon shell of the N$\mathsf{P C@C F}$ is highly hydrophilic and $0_{2}$ permeable, while the layered porous structure exposes sufficient active sites to shorten the mass transfer distance, which would promote electrocatalytic performance and increase flexibility efficiently. The obtained $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -PC@CF has an onset potential of $0.926\mathrm{~V~}$ and a halfwave potential of $0.843\mathrm{~V~}$ in the oxygen reduction reaction test, which is equivalent to commercial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{\Lambda}$ C. Most importantly, the maximum power density of the assembled ZAB is $134.7\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and it exhibits a specific capacity of $776.8\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is better than the $99.9\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}-$ based battery. An obvious improvement in the constant current discharge–charge cycle durability of the ZAB is found when compared with $\sf P t/C$ . The specific capacities of ZAB with $N//P C@C F$ as the air cathode at 5, 10 and $15\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ are 842.7, 776.8 and $715.0\mathrm{mAh}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (calculated by the mass of zinc consumed), respectively, corresponding to high energy densities of 1089.7, 977.3 and 842.2 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . A flexible solid-state battery is assembled with excellent flexibility and stability, even if the battery is folded into a large angle $(160^{\circ})$ . This work provides a new strategy for the design and synthesis of metal-free air cathodes.
