## 1. Introduction
With the increasing consumption of fossil energy, researchers are focusing on a new generation of energy technologies [1–4]. As a new type of energy technology, metal-air batteries have attracted intensive attention on account of their high energy density and pollution-free application [5–7]. Among the various metals used as the anodes of metal-air batteries, zinc has the advantages of a simple manufacturing process, abundant reserves and high safety. The theoretical energy density of Zn-air batteries (ZABs) is 1084 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ [3]. Although ZABs were commercialized in the early 20th century, the practical application of secondary ZABs remains challenging. The overpotential and slow reaction kinetics of the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) are important factors that are currently restricting the development of ZABs [8–10]. Therefore, it is necessary to develop an efficient ORR catalyst for the practical application of rechargeable ZABs. Although commercialized Pt-based electrocatalysts exhibit good catalytic activity, they are expensive and suffer from a low tolerance to methanol and poor stability [11–14].Therefore, it is of great significance to develop low-cost and high-durability catalysts for ZABs [15].
In recent years, significant research has been devoted to improving the ORR catalytic performance of non-precious and metal-free materials [16–19]. Among all kinds of non-precious metal electrocatalysts, carbon-based catalysts have the advantages of environmental protection, low cost and high stability, and are currently some of the most promising materials available [20,21]. Metal-organic frameworks are carbon-based catalysts consisting of novel framework materials connected by metal bonds with high specific surface area and controllable chemical composition [22– 24]. Zeolitic imidazolium frameworks (ZIFs) are a subclass of metal–organic frameworks that are mainly composed of transition metal ions and nitrogen-containing organic ligands. Among them, the nitrogen-rich ligand, 2-methylimidazole, of ZIF-8 can form catalytically active pyridine nitrogen and graphitic nitrogen during high temperature processes, and has therefore attracted increasing attention. The self-doped nitrogen atoms induce an inductive effect around the carbon atoms in the carbon network, which induces an electrochemical reaction with an uneven charge distribution, with obvious activity and excellent stability [25–28]. Although N doping can tune the electronic structure of adjacent carbon atoms, the configuration of N bonding and controlling the doping position and configuration in the carbon network remain challenging [29,30].
Simultaneously, the high-temperature volatilization characteristics of zinc in ZIF-8 can be used to prepare porous carbon with a microporous structure. However, the micropores generated by zinc volatilization with limited exposure of the active sites still cannot satisfy the catalytic activity of the ORR and more porous structures are therefore required to increase the specific surface area. NaCl is an inexpensive and non-polluting activator, which can assist the pyrolysis of ZIF-8 during the material preparation, with the increase in the pore structure of the material leading to improved electrochemical catalytic activity[31,32]. However, in the high-temperature calcination of ZIF-8, its own structure will inevitably be destroyed, which is not conducive to the mass transfer, thus affecting the catalytic performance of the catalyst. Therefore, the hierarchical polyvoid structure can enhance the synergistic effect of active sites and stable nanostructure,and improve the exposure of electroactive sites and the transport efficiency of the material. At present, the synthesis of most materials with layered and hollow structures is limited to various templates and is complicated with low reproducibility [33,34]. There is now an urgent need for a green and facile method to synthesize ZIFderived hierarchically porous nitrogen-doped carbon-based catalysts [17,35,36].
Here, we design a hierarchical porous carbon material with a high specific surface area and abundant pore structure as a cathode electrocatalyst for rechargeable ZABs. This hierarchical porous carbon material design strategy uses glucose as a carbon film precursor and forms a porous carbon sphere (CS) dispersion after hydrothermal treatment. And then CS combines with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ under the action of PVP. The ZIF formed by adding ligands is tightly coated by the CSs. Together, a core–shell structure is derived $(Z\mathrm{IF}@C S)$ . Subsequently, N-doped hierarchical porous carbon wrapped in porous carbon films $(N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}})$ is prepared by carbonization at high temperature using NaCl for template-assisted pyrolysis. During the pyrolysis process, the nitrogen in the organic ligands is self-doped onto the carbon substrate and transformed into porous carbon with excellent electrical conductivity (Scheme 1). The results show that the multilayered structure and porous structure have synergistic effects to improve the ORR activity, even better than the catalytic performance of commercial $\sf P t/C$ on the ORR under alkaline conditions. To demonstrate its application in energy devices, we assemble a primary ZAB with the $N-$ $\mathsf{P C@C F}$ electrocatalyst, which exhibits remarkable rechargeability and better cycling stability compared with the $\sf P t/C$ anode electrocatalyst. The flexible quasi-solid ZAB also exhibits good flexibility and stability under different bending conditions.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis of ZIF@CS
First, $60~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.3{\mathrm{~M~}}$ glucose solution was transferred to a ${90}\mathrm{mL}$ autoclave and heated at $190^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to synthesize carbon spheres dispersion. Subsequently, $0.5{\mathrm{~g~}}$ PVP was added to the above $20~\mathrm{mL}$ dispersion and stirred for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(1.190{\mathrm{g}})$ was added. the solution was diluted to $100\mathrm{mL}$ and stirred for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . The obtained suspension was mixed with $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of 2- methylimidazole $(1.313\ \mathrm{g})$ solution, stirred vigorously for $3\ \mathrm{min}$ and then kept at $40\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ for $26\mathrm{~h~}$ . The mixture $\left(Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8@\mathsf{C S}\right)$ was centrifuged and washed three times with water and ethanol, and dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}C$ (Scheme 1).
## 2.2. Synthesis of N-PC@CF
$_{0.100\mathrm{~g~}}$ ZIF- $.8@C S$ and $1.000\mathrm{~g~NaCl}$ were mixed and ground, then calcined in Ar at $950^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . After naturally cooling to room temperature, the obtained product was sonicated in ${\mathfrak{g}}_{\mathrm{mL}}$ water for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then washed with $500~\mathrm{mL}$ water to remove NaCl and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ vacuum oven, it is denoted as $N//D C@C F$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalyst characterization
As shown in Scheme 1, firstly, a uniform CS dispersion was synthesized by a hydrothermal method. $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ was then adsorbed by the PVP-functionalized CSs and the ZIF-8 derived after adding organic ligands was tightly encapsulated by the CSs to form ZIF$8@C S$ (Experimental images: Figure S1a, b and c). Finally, ZIF$8@C S$ and NaCl were calcined at high temperature to prepare catalysts with high specific surface areas and abundant electrocatalytic active sites. The melting point of crystalline NaCl is $\sim800^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ The $_{\mathrm{NaCl}}$ molten salt formed at $950~^{\circ}C$ can wrap ZIF$8@C S$ , which penetrate the carbon through a capillary action and promote the connection of carbon and the formation of mesopores[11]. In the process of pyrolysis, the volatilization of carbon and Zn in the ZIF- $8@C S$ produced pores, which can be easily occupied by the adjacent NaCl molten salt, prevent the aggregation of nanosheets[37]. After cooling to room temperature, NaCl can be easily removed by washing with water to obtain a hierarchical porous nitrogen self-doped catalyst.
Fig. 1a, b and c displays the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of uncalcined ZIF- $\cdot8@{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{S}}$ at different resolutions. ZIF8 is wrapped by CSs and still retains the regular morphology of ZIF-8, and the CSs scattered around the ZIF-8 may be due to the detachment from the ZIF-8 surface during the ultrasonic preparation of the TEM sample. Among them, the CS and ZIF morphologies in ZIF- $8@C S$ are completely consistent with their morphologies when prepared individually (Figure S2).The N-PC@CF still retains the original shape, and is wrapped with tulle-like porous carbon films through CS calcination (Fig. 1d, e and f). These CS-derived porous carbon films can not only maintain the stability of the structure during the derivatization of ZIF-8, but also act as a linker to connect multiple N-PC@CF nanoparticles into one without agglomeration (Fig. 1g, h). This interconnected structure can facilitate the rapid transfer of electrons. Moreover, N-PC@CF has a typical porous structure (Fig. 1d-f), which was caused by the escape of Zn at high temperature, as well as the vacancies formed by the removal of NaCl[17]. The element mapping images of $N//D C@C F$ show the uniform distributions of C, N, O and Zn (Fig. 1i–n). The network structure and mesopores provide a large-scale space for mass transfer so that oxygen can quickly reach the catalytic sites. The carbon layer produced after the calcination retains many of the polar oxygenation functions, there by making the material highly hydrophilic[38]. Furthermore, the porous shell can shorten the electron/reactant diffusion path[39]. The hydrophilicity test (Video 1 and 2) shows that the material exhibits high hydrophilicity and so can quickly accept oxygen in the electrolyte.
The powder X-ray diffraction patterns of N-PC@CF, NC@CF, N-PC and NC exhibit two broad peaks centered at $23.6^{\circ}$ and $43.6^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (002) and (100) lattice planes (PDF#41–1487) of the typical graphitic carbon in turbostratic carbon (Fig. 2a) [40,41]. The XRD patterns of both ZIF-8 and ZIF- $8@C S$ matched with the standard phase information of the simulation ZIF-8 (Figure S3a). Figure S3b shows the small-angle X-ray diffraction of $N//D C@C F$ NC@CF, N-PC and NC. Graphitization of the carbon in the catalyst can improve the corrosion resistance and conductivity in the electrocatalytic process[42]. No diffraction peak of Zn was observed in the X-ray diffraction pattern, indicating that most of the Zn was volatilized. We also performed inductively coupled plasma analysis on the N-PC@CF and N-PC to verify the presence of Zn in their carbon matrix. The inductively coupled plasma analysis demonstrated that the Zn contents of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ and N-PC were $1.48\%$ and $2.22\%$ , respectively. Previous studies have shown that residual $Z\mathrm{n}$ has no catalytic effect on the ORR and NaCl can promote the evaporation of $Z{\mathrm{n}}[17]$ .
The disordered structure and defect density can be obtained from the Raman spectra (Fig. 2d)[43]. The D-band corresponding to $\sim1352~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to defect and disordered bands, mainly corresponding to vacancies and heteroatoms on the graphite plane, while the G-band corresponding to $\sim1586~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to the band stretching of the $\scriptstyle s p^{2}$ bond[44]. N-PC@CF and N-PC activated by NaCl have a greater degree of defects than ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ and NC, indicating that the activator increased the defect level. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of N-PC@CF was higher than that of N-PC, meaning that more edge and topological defects were formed in $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ [45]. These abundant defect sites can regulate the electronic and surface properties of N-PC@CF and make it active in the catalytic process. The Raman spectra of CPF and CF is consistent with the above analysis (Figure S4)[46].
The chemical environment and bonding configurations of different elements were further investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The related characteristic peaks of C, N, O and $Z\mathrm{n}$ can be observed from the XPS spectra of N-PC@CF, ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ ， N-PC, and NC (Figure S5). The C1s spectrum can be resolved into four peaks by defolding integration (Fig. 2c and Table S1). The main peak at $284.4\pm0.1$ eV corresponds to $s p^{2}$ hybrid carbon, while the peak centered at $285.3\pm0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to $\ensuremath{s p}^{3}$ hybrid carbon [40,47]. According to the ratio of the corresponding peak area to the total peak area of $\textsf{C1s}$ the carbon content of the surface $\ensuremath{s p}^{3}$ and $\scriptstyle s p^{2}$ states can be calculated. The $s p^{3}/s p^{2}$ ratios of $N//D C@C F$ ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ , N-PC and NC were 0.41, 0.48, 0.38 and 0.42, respectively. A small $\scriptstyle s p^{2}$ hybrid carbon content could lead to insufficient conductivity[47]. The third peak at $286.3\pm0.1$ eV and the corresponding $\mathsf{C-N}$ was formed by the pyrolysis of the ZIF framework[40]. The N 1 s spectrum can be deconvoluted into pyridinic N ${\bf398.2\pm}$ $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ ), pyrrolic N $(399.7\pm0.2\:\mathrm{eV})$ , graphitic N $\langle400.8\pm0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) and oxidized N $(402.7\pm0.2\:\mathrm{eV})$ (Fig. 2d)[48]. Pyridinic N and graphitic N were considered to be catalytically active sites in $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon[49,50]. The $\mathsf{N a C l}$ introduced during the pyrolysis process increases the relative content of pyridine N (Table S2). The pyridine N atom has a strong electron accepting ability and contributes an electron to the conjugated $\pi$ -bond system, which can generate a positive charge on adjacent carbon atoms. This characteristic facilitates the adsorption of oxygen and accelerates the ORR process [51].
In order to further evaluate the porosity, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption desorption measurements were carried out. The samples possess significant characteristics of the type-IV profile (Fig. 3a), indicating that N-PC@CF, NC@CF, N-PC and NC possess microporous and mesoporous characteristics[52]. The specific surface area of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ , ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ , N-PC and NC were 708.05, 212.94, 869.21 and $234.17~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. The specific surface areas of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ and N-PC were greatly improved after NaCl activation (Fig. 3b). Moreover, $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ and N-PC mainly increase the area of the micropores. The large specific surface area can expose more active sites and the increased pore structure also provides a fast channel for the mass transfer of oxygen. Fig. 3c shows that the structures of the four materials mainly consisted of micropore and mesopore, which was mainly caused by the volatilization of Zn[53]. Moreover, $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ and ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ possess larger average pore diameters of 7.9 and $9.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ , as exhibited in Table S3. N-PC@CF and ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ have obvious mesoporous structures at $10{-}20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 3c). These mesopores may exist at the interface between the CSs and ZIF. These mesopores can increase the effective mass transfer of oxygen and allow it and its intermediates to reach the catalytic sites quickly [54].
## 3.2. Electrocatalytic performance evaluation
For the purpose of exploring the catalytic activity, we conducted a performance evaluation through electrochemical tests. In the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ saturated electrolyte, the N-PC@CF has no obvious redox peak, while there is an obvious reduction peak in the $0_{2}$ saturated electrolyte (Fig. 4a). Figure S6 shows the CV images of other materials in $0_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ . The excellent performance of the $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ was also demonstrated by the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curve (Fig. 4b). The LSV curve of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ exhibits the most positive onset potential $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{onset}}\sim0.926\mathrm{~V},$ ), halfwave potential $(E_{1/2}\sim0.81$
8 V) and limiting current density $(5.71~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2};$ , which was better than for 20 wt% Pt/C $E_{\mathrm{onset}}\sim0.925\$ V, $E_{1/2}\sim0.818\:\ V$ , $5.63\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ). In addition, the LSV curves showed that the activity of N-PC@CF was higher than that of N-PC. Although the apparent nitrogen content of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ was lower compared with N-PC, the more effective N was exposed to the $s p^{3}/s p^{2}$ carbon interface[47]. Furthermore, N-PC@CF has more carbon defects and mesopores than $N\mathrm{.}$ -PC, which are beneficial to increase the mass transfer rate and promote the ORR process[55]. The catalytic activity of $N\cdot$ $\mathsf{P C@C F}$ , N-PC and CPF was higher than that of ${\mathsf{N C@C F}}$ , NC and CF, revealing that the samples activated by NaCl have better ORR performance. This is because the NaCl-activated samples have more defects and larger specific surface area[17]. The resulting N$\mathsf{P C@C F}$ presented a lower Tafel slope $\langle55\mathrm{mV}/\mathrm{dec}\rangle$ compared with that of 20 wt% $\sf P t/C$ (84 mV/dec) and other as-prepared catalysts (Fig. 5c), indicating that N-PC@CF possesses more ideal ORR kinetics[36]. The ORR performance of N-PC@CF in alkaline media is compared with that of metal-free catalysts reported recently (Table S4)..
The LSV curves was used to study the electron transfer kinetics of N-PC@CF at different rotating speeds (Figure S7a). According to Koutecky–Levich (K-L) equations (1) and (2), K-L plots at different potentials were obtained. The K-L plots of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ (Figure S7b) with good linearity reflected first-order reaction kinetics related to the $0_{2}$ concentration. The electron transfer number (n) of N$\mathsf{P C@C F}$ is $\sim4$ , illustrating that N-PC@CF mainly follows the fourelectron transfer path when performing ORR electrocatalysis. LSV curves and corresponding K-L diagrams for other materials at different rotation rates (Figure S7c-p).
In order to study the conductivity and charge transfer kinetics, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measurements were performed in a 0.1 M KOH solution (Fig. 4d). The impedance result was fitted according to the equivalent circuit diagram (Table S5). Obviously, N-PC@CF has the smallest charge transfer resistance, indicating that the higher the conductivity in the ORR process, the faster the reaction kinetics. A low charge transfer resistance is also one of the main factors contributing to the excellent performance of $N\cdot$
PC@CF, which is consistent with a previous electrochemical analysis[56].
Methanol tolerance and stability are also two important factors for evaluating the performance of the catalyst. Fig. 4e illustrates that $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ retained better methanol resistance than $20\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\sf P t/C$ . After adding $1\mathrm{~M~}$ methanol, the performance of $N//D C@C F$ was barely affected. In contrast, 20 wt% $\sf P t/C$ was greatly affected and the current decreased by nearly $20\%$ . The stability of $N\cdot$ $\mathsf{P C@C F}$ was further demonstrated by the durability tests of $N\mathrm{.}$ - $\mathsf{P C@C F}$ and $20~{\mathsf{w t\%}}~{\mathsf{P t/C}}$ on $2\mathrm{~M~}$ and $3\mathrm{~M~}$ methanol (Figures S8a, b). After 5000 cycles, the limiting current of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ and $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\sf P t/C$ both dropped by $\sim6.9\%$ (Fig. 4f), the half-wave potential of 20 wt% $\sf P t/C$ dropped by $20\mathrm{\mV}$ while $N//D C@C F$ dropped by $3~\mathrm{mV}$ , which still has better ORR activity than $20~{\mathsf{w t\%}}~{\mathsf{P t/C}}$ . The inorganic carbon in the carbon shell of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ plays an important role in improving its methanol resistance and stability [57].
## 3.3. Zn-air battery test
Finally, the $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ electrocatalyst was used as the air cathode of a ZAB with $\sf P t/C$ also used as a comparison[32]. A schematic diagram of the assembled battery is given in Fig. 5a. The open circuit voltage of the N-PC@CF-based ZAB is $1.45\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. 5b and inset). In addition, the power density of the N-PC@CF-based ZAB is 134.7 mW $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is better than the $99.9\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ of the $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}-$ based battery (Fig. 5c)[58]. Fig. 5d shows the discharge curves at 5, 10 and $15\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ . The specific capacities of ZAB with $N-$ $\mathsf{P C@C F}$ as the air cathode at 5, 10 and $15\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ (calculated by the mass of zinc consumed) are 842.7, 776.8 and $715.0\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively, corresponding to high energy densities of 1089.7, 977.3 and 842.2 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5e). At a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , the specific capacity is comparable to $768\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ of $\sf P t/C$ (Fig. 5f) (equations (4) and (5)). Compared with the $\sf P t/C$ energy density of 1017 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , the energy density has room for improvement (Figure S9a)[59].
Figure S9b describes the N-PC@CF ZAB air cathode, the voltage is stable under different current densities and has excellent rate performance. Compared with the $\sf P t/C$ -based ZAB, the charging voltage of the $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ battery is lower in the entire current density range and the voltage gap between charging and discharging is smaller. In the test system of LAND, $100\mathrm{h}$ cyclic charge–discharge curves of N-PC@CF and $\sf P t/C$ with a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ are presented in Fig. 5g. The current density is $5\ \mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and the $200\mathrm{~h~}$ charge–discharge curve of the N-PC@CF-based battery (1 h per cycle) is given in Fig. 5h. The secondary charge–discharge potential difference $(\xi)$ and round-trip efficiency (e) of the
$N$ -PC@CF-based ZAB are $0.84\mathrm{~V~}$ and $57\%$ in the 20th cycle, and $0.9\mathrm{V}$ and $54\%$ at the 200th cycle (Figure S9c,d).. The experimental results show that the catalytic activity and stability of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ as an air cathode are better than $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}[15,60]$ . This performance is comparable to that of previously published metal-free air electrode catalysts (Table S6).
As our demand for flexible energy storage equipment continues to grow, all-solid-state ZABs are expected to become a wearable and sustainable alternative energy source[61]. Load N-PC@CF on foamed nickel as an air cathode, zinc foil as an anode and gel electrolyte as a conductive layer to assemble a solid-state flexible battery (Fig. 6a). According to the discharge curve, the peak power density can be calculated to be $58\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}$ (Fig. 6b). The multimeter tested a relatively stable open circuit voltage of 1.321 V (Fig. 6c). More importantly, the foldability test in Fig. 6d shows that even if the battery is folded into a large angle $(160^{\circ})$ , it has excellent stability. Using the LAND system, the charge–discharge curve of ZAB was tested. The current density is $2\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a good charging and discharging platform is maintained after 50 cycles (Fig. 6e, the inset is a partial enlarged view of the 5th and 50th). Integrating it into a series circuit (Fig. 6f), two solid-state ZABs connected in series can also power six LEDs, showing good mechanical stability and excellent battery performance[15,28,62].
## 4. Conclusions
In short, it is different from traditional synthetic strategies to enhance electrocatalytic activity, such as porous nanostructures [32]and self-doping[6]. We have used a simple, green method to synthesize metal–organic framework-derived hierarchically porous nitrogen self-doped N-PC@CF. N-PC@CF shows excellent catalytic activity, which is comparable to 20 wt% Pt/C. As an efficient electrocatalyst, the prepared $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ also exhibits strong methanol tolerance and stability. Assembled ZABs also have high specific capacity and excellent cycle stability and flexible solidstate batteries have flexibility and mechanical stability. The excellent battery performance of $N{\mathrm{-PC@CF}}$ is mainly due to the synergy of its unique characteristics[43]: (1) the carbon shell improved hydrophilicity and provided larger mesopores; (2) hierarchical porous structure provides shorter $0_{2}$ diffusion paths and accelerated mass transfer; (3) the uniform nitrogen self doping and high specific surface area exposed more active sites; (4) low charge transfer resistance accelerated electron transfer. Performance tests show that this graded carbon material as ZABs assembled with air cathodes has strong competitiveness in future energy conversion and electronic energy storage applications.
